# Thinking Out Loud — Echo Brief

A long, unstructured voice note is not a spec. Before building anything from it,
echo it back as a dense brief that separates **decided** from **open** from
**your own fills** — so the requester can correct you in seconds instead of
discovering your guesses after the work is done.

**Core principle:** the brief contains only what the ramble said, plus a clearly
labelled section for everything **you** added. The requester reads the labelled
section first. Anything you silently resolved is a bug.

**Why this exists:** a ten-minute ramble holds maybe five real decisions and
three unanswered questions. Without the echo pass, the unanswered questions get
filled by the builder, invisibly, and the build is judged against a spec the
requester never wrote.

## When to Use

- A voice note, long chat dump, or rambling brief arrives as the task input.
- A live call ends and you are about to act on it (`live-meeting-attendance`,
  `discovery-call-prep`).
- The requester says "you know what I mean", "just build it", "some kind of X".
- Any input where the same topic flips direction mid-note (Slack then email,
  free then paid) — the flip must be recorded, not silently resolved.
- Before a multi-hour build, a client site, or an agent run: the echo costs one
  message and saves the whole lane.

**Not for:** a written, structured brief (already ordered — just execute), or a
single clear instruction like "restart the service".

## How to Run

No script. It is a contract you follow on the first reply, using
`references/echo-format.md` (template + worked example).

1. Listen/read the whole input once without planning anything.
2. Extract into five sections: **Mission**, **Locked**, **Open**, **Ledger**,
   **My additions**.
3. Send it as the reply, then the closing line, then stop and wait.

Closing line: "Correct anything that's off, especially My additions. Want the
interview for the Open items?"

## Rules

- **One line per item.** Nothing appears twice; no recap of what the requester
  said ("you said…" is noise — everything outside My additions is theirs).
- **Quantifiers go to Open or Guessed.** "usual", "soon", "standard", "a
  proper one" never silently become a locked value.
- **Flips are recorded, not resolved.** Second position wins, and the first is
  logged as `Flipped:` so the requester can un-flip it.
- **Guess nothing silently.** Inferred (implied but unstated) and Guessed (a gap
  you filled) are both listed under My additions, most-load-bearing first.
- **Mark the stated priority** with **top priority** — the ramble usually has
  one, and it is what everything else is traded against.
- **Do not start work in the same reply.** The echo is the deliverable; the only
  permitted next step is the interview for the Open items.
- **Length:** well under 250 words for a ten-minute ramble. Dense, no padding.
- **Language:** mirror the requester's chat language for the brief (with this
  user, Hinglish is normal). Any **file** deliverable written afterwards is pure
  English, per the user's standing rule.
- **Brand/safety:** the brief may name a client internally, but the echo goes to
  the channel it came from; never blend two clients' material, and never paste
  credentials, tokens, or phone numbers that were spoken aloud.

## Output contract

```markdown
## Mission
<one sentence: what is actually being achieved>

## Locked (decisions and constraints)
- <one line each; mark the stated priority **top priority**>

## Open (needs an answer)
- <question the ramble raised but never answered>

## Ledger
- Flipped: <position A>, then <position B>. Going with B.
- Parked: <tangent in one line>

## My additions (correct these first)
- Inferred: <implied but never stated>
- Guessed: <gap I filled>
```

## Pitfalls

- **Treating the echo as a summary.** A summary compresses; the echo *separates
  ownership*. If the requester cannot point at exactly what you invented, it
  failed.
- **Over-locking.** Promoting a vague idea to Locked makes you look decisive and
  builds the wrong thing. Vague goes to Open.
- **Dropping the tangent.** Parked items are cheap and often return. One line.
- **Building after the echo without an answer.** Silence is not consent; wait
  for the correction or an explicit "go".
- **Answering your own Open item** because the answer "seems obvious". That is
  the exact failure this skill exists to prevent.
- **Padding with politeness.** No preamble, no "great idea!", no emojis in the
  brief.

## Verification

Before sending, check the echo against the input:

- Every Locked line is quotable back to something the requester said.
- Every fill, assumption, or timezone/unit decision appears in My additions.
- Every contradiction is in Ledger as `Flipped:`, not resolved silently.
- Word count under 250; no item appears in two sections.
- You ended with the closing line and did not start the work.

